moddat <- dist_turn[, c("turnout", "turnoutLag2", "turnoutLag1","year", "contest",
                        "fallTurnout", "VAP_adjLOG", "distid", "districtwide",
                        "per_white_all", "per65o", "PerBachelorOrAbove", "OOH_share", 
                        "median_incomeLOG", "minBlaisLago", "contest")]
#moddat <- na.omit(moddat)
moddat <- subset(moddat, turnout < 0.95,)
moddat <- moddat[order(moddat$distid, moddat$year),]
moddat$distid <- factor(moddat$distid)
moddat$year <- factor(moddat$year)
moddat$contest <- factor(moddat$contest)
moddat$turnout <- moddat$turnout * 100
moddat$districtwide <- factor(moddat$districtwide)
moddat$VAP_adjLOG_sq <- moddat$VAP_adjLOG^2

nums <- sapply(moddat, is.numeric)
ScaleMeans <- apply(moddat[, nums], 2, mean, na.rm=TRUE)
ScaleSD <- apply(moddat[, nums], 2, sd, na.rm=TRUE)
moddatScale <- cbind(ScaleMeans, ScaleSD); rm(ScaleMeans, ScaleSD)
#Rescale
library(arm)
nums <- sapply(moddat, is.numeric)
moddat[, nums] <- apply(moddat[, nums], 2, rescale)


naive1 <- lm(turnout ~ turnoutLag2 + fallTurnout + VAP_adjLOG + VAP_adjLOG_sq + 
               per_white_all + per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove + OOH_share + 
               median_incomeLOG + districtwide + minBlaisLago,
             data = moddat)

autoplot(naive1)

@

Figure \ref{fig:simpleModel} shows regression diagnostics for this simple multiple 
regression. The key result here is that there are a number of high leverage 
observations that appear to be extreme outliers. Additionally, the upper left 
quadrant of Figure \ref{fig:simpleModel} suggests the presence of some level of 
heteroskedacticity in the results, a likely result of the clustering and repeated 
observation features of the dataset. The upper right quadrant shows the QQ plot 
for this regression model which demonstrates that the model deviates from normality 
quite substantially. I investigate this further by looking for extreme outliers 
in the turnout variable. 

<<outlierdetect, results='hide'>>=
centr <- mean(moddat$turnout)
stddev <- sd(moddat$turnout)
moddat$outflag <- (moddat$turnout - centr) / stddev
@

There are \Sexpr{table(moddat$outflag>5)[[2]]} cases where 
the value of the dependent variable is more than five standard deviations above 
the group average. This is less than half of 1\% of the observations and is unlikely 
to be influential, however, it is also likely that such deviations are due to 
measurement error either in the VAP denominator or in the aggregation of district 
board election vote counts themselves. I opt to remove these cases in the analyses 
that follow. In addition, I adopt the strategies outlined in 
Subsection \ref{subsec:spec} - year fixed effects, district fixed effects, and 
clustered standard errors to provide a more robust view of the effects in school 
board turnout. The results for these alternative specifications are displayed 
alongside the initial model in Table \ref{tab:lagmods}. 

\clearpage
\section{Results}

I now review the results for models for all of the theories discussed above -- 
habit forming voters, rational voters, pivotal voters, and social group motivated 
voters. I fit both a fixed-effect OLS regression and a mixed-effect linear 
model for each of these theories including control variables and the key predictors. 
I fist look at evidence for these theories in each of these models for an effect 
on turnout in the spring school board races. Then I apply these same models to 
the rolloff of voters in the spring election. Finally, I conclude by interpreting 
the substantive effect of these variables to weigh how large the effects of 
these characteristics might be on school board election turnout. 

\subsection{Habit Forming Voters}

<<lagmodRLM, results ='hide'>>=
