Table \ref{tab:meTurnout} confirms the theoretical expectations from above to a 
large extent. 

% Lagged turnout increases turnout
% district size decreases turnout
% whiter communities vote slightly less, not sig
% older communities vote more, sig.
% education vote more, not sig. 
% OOH does not appear to matter
% income leads to more voting, except with fall turnout, is sig.
% district wide dummy does not matter
% less competitive the seat, the less turnout (higher BL = less turnout)
% Fall turnout related to spring turnout
% squared relationship with VAP?

% Footnote about fixed effect models
% in non district fixed effects, relationship is same with lag1 and lag2
% District FE sign reverses and lagged turnout is related to negative turnout
%% Reversion to the mean, small within district differences, small within district samples
%% Lose a lot of statistical significance because the models seem to fall apart



Prior turnout appears to be a substantively important predictor in Table \ref{tab:lagmods}.\footnote{Using twice-lagged school board turnout. Results are not substantively different 
when I estimate using single lagged school board turnout, as shown in Table \ref{tab:lagmods2}.} 
Model 1 represents a standard pooled regression with clustered standard errors to 
correct for the violation of the IID assumptions of OLS. The key independent variable is 
twice lagged turnout for school board elections, which represents our test of habit 
forming in school board elections. %RESULTS OF ONCE LAGGED AVAILALE IN APPENDIX 
Control variables included are some standard demographic predictors of voter turnout including per 
capita income, education level, percent of population over 65, percent of population 
that is white. Additionally, a school district specific control, the share of residents 
in owner-occupied housing is included to reflect the tight coupling between school boards 
and property ownership. 

Only in the district fixed effect specification does the effect of prior turnout 
disappear. However, there are many reasons to doubt the validity of this specification. 
First, the district fixed effects are overly conservative as many of the key underlining 
predictors are not highly variable year to year within districts. Second, there are 
other signs of specification problems including highly inflated standard errors for 
other parameters and highly inflated coefficients for other parameters. The district estimates themselves are problematic, the model is likely underpowered to fit them properly and 
they are likely too sensitive to outliers and therefore overfit. There are two 
approaches to adjusting for this -- penalized estimation or partial pooling approach using 
a mixed effect model. With over 300 
grouping parameters to estimate, it is better to turn to a partial pooling approach that 
allows estimates of the district effect to be shrunk toward the pooled grand estimate 
in order to avoid overfit. Partial pooling 
models can be fit in R using the \texttt{lme4} package \citep{lme4, gelhill}. 
I employ the same specification as the fixed effect model before, but now model 
the district effects as random effects in order to avoid overfit. The results 
appear in Table \ref{tab:meTurnout} and appear much in line with the results 
from the models in Table \ref{tab:lagmods}, but now also directly model the 
clustered structure of the district terms. 


