\begin{tiny}
<<lagModMM, results ='asis'>>=
library(lme4)

mixedModlags <- lmer(turnout ~ turnoutLag2 +  VAP_adjLOG + 
                    per_white_all + 
                       per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove + OOH_share + median_incomeLOG + 
               districtwide + 
               minBlaisLago + (1|year) + (1|distid), 
              data = moddat, REML = FALSE)

mixedMod1lagsb <- lmer(turnout ~ turnoutLag2 + fallTurnout + VAP_adjLOG + VAP_adjLOG_sq +
                    per_white_all + 
                       per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove + OOH_share + median_incomeLOG + 
               districtwide + 
               minBlaisLago + (1|year) + (1|distid), 
              data = moddat, REML = FALSE)

coefnamesLag3 <- c("Intercept","Lagged Turnout (2)", "ln(VAP)", 
                 "\\% White", 
                  "\\% Over 65", "\\% Bach. +", "\\% OOH", 
                  "ln(MedianIncome)", "District-wide", "Blais-Lago", 
                  "Fall Turnout",  "$ln(VAP)^{2}$")
tabnote <- "Model includes both year and district random effects modeled as intercepts."

apsrtable(mixedModlags, mixedMod1lagsb, caption = "Partial Pooling Model of Lagged Turnout",
          model.names = c("No Fall Turnout", "Fall Turnout"), 
            label = "tab:meTurnout",  
           notes = list("All variables grand-mean scaled and centered.", 
                       stars.note), 
          Sweave = FALSE, digits = 3, coef.names = coefnamesLag3)
@
\end{tiny}


<<laggedTurnSens, results='hide', eval=TRUE>>=
library(RLRsim)
# m0 <- lm(turnout ~ turnoutLag2 + fallTurnout + VAP_adjLOG + 
#                     per_white_all + turnoutLag2 * VAP_adjLOG + 
#                        per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove + OOH_share + median_incomeLOG + 
#                districtwide + 
#                minBlaisLago, 
#          data = moddat) # fit the null model
# 
# exactLRT(m = mixedMod1, m0 = m0)

# Test for year specific slopes
m.slope <- update(mixedMod1lagsb, . ~ . - (1|year))
m0 <- update(mixedMod1lagsb, . ~ . - (1|distid))
lagLRT <- exactRLRT(m.slope, mixedMod1lagsb, m0)
lagLRT$callFull <- mixedMod1lagsb@call
lagLRT$callSmall <- m.slope@call
@

The proper model of the random effects is important. Using the \texttt{RLRsim} package 
in R, I test the specification of the random effects using an exact likelihood 
ratio test and an exact restricted likelihood ratio test \cite{RLRsim}. The first 
test is used to examine whether the random effects are the appropriate specification. 
The restricted likelihood ratio test is used to compare two nested models with 
different random effect specifications to determine the appropriate specification. 
In this case, a model with random effects for district and year was compared against 
a model with random effects only for district. In both cases, the additional 
random effect terms significantly improve model fit and in Table \ref{tab:meTurnout} 
this final model is what is shown. 


