\subsection{Rational Voters}

My second empirical question was to examine the relationship between electorate size 
and turnout, and electorate size, margin of victory and turnout. I use an adjusted estimate 
of the voting age population - the denominator in the turnout variable - to serve as 
an estimate of the electorate size. This is a conservative estimate of the actual electorate 
size in school board elections, which as we have seen can be as low as 15\% of the voting 
age population. If we assume that voters adjust the electorate size equally across jurisdictions 
when making their assessment of the perceived uiltity of voting, then this is not a factor. 
If we have evidence that voters in different jurisdicstions have more information about the 
likely electorate size in a school board election, and this varies across jurisdictions, 
then this becomes more problematic. 

<<closeModRLM, results ='asis'>>=
moddat <- dist_turn[, c("turnout", "turnoutLag2", "year", "fallTurnout", 
                        "VAP_adjLOG", "distid", 
                        "per_white_all", "per65o", "PerBachelorOrAbove", 
                        "OOH_share", "median_incomeLOG", "districtwide", 
                        "contest", "minBlaisLago")]
moddat <- na.omit(moddat)
moddat <- moddat[order(moddat$distid, moddat$year),]
moddat <- moddat[order(moddat$distid, moddat$year),]
moddat$distid <- factor(moddat$distid)
moddat$year <- factor(moddat$year)
moddat$turnout <- moddat$turnout * 100
moddat$districtwide <- factor(moddat$districtwide)
moddat$contest <- factor(moddat$contest)
moddat$VAP_adjLOG_sq <- moddat$VAP_adjLOG^2
centr <- mean(moddat$turnout)
stddev <- sd(moddat$turnout)
moddat$outflag <- (moddat$turnout - centr) / stddev
moddat <- moddat[moddat$outflag <= 5 & moddat$outflag >= -5,]

nums <- sapply(moddat, is.numeric)
ScaleMeans <- apply(moddat[, nums], 2, mean, na.rm=TRUE)
ScaleSD <- apply(moddat[, nums], 2, sd, na.rm=TRUE)
moddatScale <- cbind(ScaleMeans, ScaleSD); rm(ScaleMeans, ScaleSD)
nums <- sapply(moddat, is.numeric)
moddat[, nums] <- apply(moddat[, nums], 2, rescale)

dd <<- datadist(moddat)
options(datadist="dd")

closeRLM <- ols(turnout ~ VAP_adjLOG + per_white_all + 
                  per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove + OOH_share + median_incomeLOG +
                  districtwide + minBlaisLago, 
              data = moddat, x = TRUE, y = TRUE)

closeRLM$se <- sqrt(diag(vcov(robcov(closeRLM, cluster = moddat$distid))))
closeRLM2 <- ols(turnout ~ VAP_adjLOG + VAP_adjLOG_sq + per_white_all + 
                  per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove + OOH_share + median_incomeLOG +
                   districtwide + minBlaisLago + factor(year),
              data = moddat, x= TRUE, y = TRUE)
closeRLM2$se <- sqrt(diag(vcov(robcov(closeRLM2, cluster = moddat$distid))))
closeRLM3 <- ols(turnout ~ VAP_adjLOG + VAP_adjLOG_sq+ per_white_all + 
                  per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove + OOH_share + median_incomeLOG +
                   districtwide + minBlaisLago + factor(year) + factor(distid), 
              data = moddat, x = TRUE, y = TRUE)
closeRLM3$se <- sqrt(diag(vcov(robcov(closeRLM3, cluster = moddat$distid))))
closeRLM4 <- ols(turnout ~ VAP_adjLOG + VAP_adjLOG_sq+ minBlaisLago*VAP_adjLOG + per_white_all + 
                  per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove + OOH_share + median_incomeLOG +
                   districtwide + minBlaisLago + factor(year) + factor(distid), 
              data = moddat, x = TRUE, y = TRUE)
closeRLM4$se <- sqrt(diag(vcov(robcov(closeRLM4, cluster = moddat$distid))))


closeCoefs1 <- c("Intercept", "ln(VAP)", 
                  "$ln(VAP)^{2}$", "\\% White", 
                  "\\% Over 65", "\\% Bach. +", "\\% OOH", 
                  "ln(MedianIncome)", "District-wide", "Blais-Lago", 
                 "VAP x Blais-Lago")

closeModTab <- apsrtable(closeRLM3, closeRLM4, se="robust", 
          label="tab:closemods", 
          model.names=c("RLM + District FE", "Interact + Year FE"), 
          caption = "Models of Competitiveness and Turnout in School Board Elections",
          notes = list(se.note, stars.note, "Year and district fixed effects omitted."),
          omitcoef=list("distid", 
                        expression(grep("distid", coefnames, value=TRUE)), 
                        "year", 
                        expression(grep("year", coefnames, value = TRUE))),
          coef.names = closeCoefs1,
          Sweave=FALSE)
@


<<cleanupa, echo=FALSE,results='hide'>>=
rm(ADMIN, VAP_dist, dist.tmp, dvp, errors, plotdf, presTurn, canInterp,
   canInterpIDX, centr, dumbRLM1, dumbRLM1b, dumbRLM2, dumbRLM2b, dumbRLMbest, 
   dumbRLMbestb, i, j)
@


Table \ref{tab:meClose} shows the partial-pooling results for the competitiveness 
of elections. Variables are the same pretty much from table \ref{tab:meTurnout}. 
These models include two separate measures of the closeness of elections. The first 
builds on the coding schema in Chapter \ref{chap:candidacy} to label races as 
contested or uncontested based on the presence of candidates. As expected, uncontested 
races have much lower turnout than contested races. Races contested as open seats 
or by incumbents have no detectable difference on turnout. 

Again, the Blais-Lago quotient matters and is in the expected direction -- less 
competitive elections result in less turnout. However, the expectation that the 
relationship between competitiveness and district size would have an effect on 
turnout is not confirmed here. \footnote{In the fixed effect specifications, the 
same specification problems from Table \ref{tab:lagmods} appaers in Table 
\ref{tab:closemods}. Again, I turn to partial pooling to resolve this issue.}

\begin{tiny}
<<closeModMM, results='asis'>>=
mixedModclose <- lmer(turnout ~  minBlaisLago + VAP_adjLOG + VAP_adjLOG_sq +
                    minBlaisLago*VAP_adjLOG + per_white_all + 
                    per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove + OOH_share + 
                    median_incomeLOG + districtwide + 
                  contest  + (1|distid), 
              data = moddat, REML = TRUE)

mixedModcloseB <- lmer(turnout ~  VAP_adjLOG +  VAP_adjLOG_sq + 
                     fallTurnout +
                     per_white_all + 
                     per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove + OOH_share + 
                     median_incomeLOG + districtwide + contest + 
                   (1|year) + (1|distid), 
              data = moddat, REML = TRUE)

mixedModcloseC <- update(mixedModcloseB, . ~ . - contest + 
                           minBlaisLago * VAP_adjLOG)

closeCoefs2 <- c("Intercept", "ln(VAP)", 
                  "$ln(VAP)^{2}$", "Fall Turnout", "\\% White", 
                  "\\% Over 65", "\\% Bach. +", "\\% OOH", 
                  "ln(MedianIncome)", "District-wide", "Open Contested", 
                 "Uncontested", "Blais-Lago", "VAP x Blais-Lago")


# summary(mixedMod2)
apsrtable(mixedModcloseB, mixedModcloseC, caption = "Partial Pooling Models of Competitiveness",
          model.names = c("Qualitative", "Blais-Lago"),
            label = "tab:meClose", notes = list(tabnote, stars.note), 
          coef.names = closeCoefs2, 
          Sweave = FALSE)
@
\end{tiny}


<<closeModSens,results='hide', eval=TRUE>>=
m0 <- lm(turnout ~  minBlaisLago + VAP_adjLOG + minBlaisLago*VAP_adjLOG + 
           per_white_all + per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove + OOH_share + 
           median_incomeLOG + districtwide + 
                  contest, data = moddat) # fit the null model

exactLRT(m = refitML(mixedModclose), m0 = m0)

# Test for year specific slopes
m.slope <- update(mixedModcloseB, . ~ . - (1|year))
m0 <- update(mixedModcloseB, . ~ . - (1|distid))
closeLRT <- exactRLRT(m.slope, mixedModcloseB, m0)
closeLRT$callFull <- mixedModcloseB@call
closeLRT$callSmall <- m.slope@call
@

\subsection{Social Groups}

To test the argument that voter turnout may be influenced by the share of the 
electorate that is made up of unionized teachers, I combine features from the 
previous models in an attempt to find evidence of such an effect.  

% Partisanship and unionization

\subsubsection{Teachers' Unions}
<<teachInflRLM, results ='hide'>>=
moddat <- dist_turn[, c("turnout", "turnoutLag2", "year", "fallTurnout", 
                        "VAP_adjLOG", "distid", "per_white_all", "per65o", 
                        "PerBachelorOrAbove", "OOH_share", "median_incomeLOG", 
                        "teachShareofVoters", "districtwide", 
                        "contest", "minBlaisLago")]
moddat <- na.omit(moddat)
moddat <- moddat[order(moddat$distid, moddat$year),]
moddat$distid <- factor(moddat$distid)
moddat$year <- factor(moddat$year)
moddat <- moddat[order(moddat$distid, moddat$year),]
moddat$distid <- factor(moddat$distid)
moddat$contest <- factor(moddat$contest)
moddat$districtwide <- factor(moddat$districtwide)
moddat$VAP_adjLOG_sq <- moddat$VAP_adjLOG^2
moddat$year <- factor(moddat$year)
moddat$turnout <- moddat$turnout * 100
centr <- mean(moddat$turnout)
stddev <- sd(moddat$turnout)
moddat$outflag <- (moddat$turnout - centr) / stddev
moddat <- moddat[moddat$outflag <=5 & moddat$outflag >= -5,]

nums <- sapply(moddat, is.numeric)
ScaleMeans <- apply(moddat[, nums], 2, mean, na.rm=TRUE)
ScaleSD <- apply(moddat[, nums], 2, sd, na.rm=TRUE)
moddatScale <- cbind(ScaleMeans, ScaleSD); rm(ScaleMeans, ScaleSD)
nums <- sapply(moddat, is.numeric)
moddat[, nums] <- apply(moddat[, nums], 2, rescale)
dd <<- datadist(moddat)
options(datadist="dd")

teachRLM <- ols(turnout ~ teachShareofVoters + fallTurnout + minBlaisLago + 
                    VAP_adjLOG + per_white_all + per65o + 
                  PerBachelorOrAbove + OOH_share + median_incomeLOG + 
                  districtwide + contest, data = moddat, x = TRUE, y = TRUE)

teachRLM$se <- sqrt(diag(vcov(robcov(teachRLM, cluster = moddat$distid))))

teachRLM2 <- ols(turnout ~ teachShareofVoters + fallTurnout + minBlaisLago + 
                    VAP_adjLOG + per_white_all + per65o + 
                   PerBachelorOrAbove + OOH_share + median_incomeLOG + 
                   districtwide + contest + year,
              data = moddat, x= TRUE, y = TRUE)

teachRLM2$se <- sqrt(diag(vcov(robcov(teachRLM2, cluster = moddat$distid))))


teachRLM3 <- ols(turnout ~ teachShareofVoters + fallTurnout + minBlaisLago + VAP_adjLOG +
                   VAP_adjLOG_sq + per_white_all + per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove +
                   OOH_share + median_incomeLOG + districtwide + contest + 
                   distid + factor(year), 
              data = moddat, x = TRUE, y = TRUE)

teachRLM3$se <- sqrt(diag(vcov(robcov(teachRLM3, cluster = moddat$distid))))

# Interaction model
teachRLM4 <- ols(turnout ~ teachShareofVoters + fallTurnout + minBlaisLago + 
                   VAP_adjLOG +  VAP_adjLOG_sq +
                     + per_white_all + per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove +
                   OOH_share + median_incomeLOG + districtwide + contest + 
                   teachShareofVoters * VAP_adjLOG + factor(year) +
                   distid, 
              data = moddat, x = TRUE, y = TRUE)

teachRLM4$se <- sqrt(diag(vcov(robcov(teachRLM4, cluster = moddat$distid))))


teachCoefnames <- c("Intercept", "Teacher \\% VAP", "Fall Turnout", 
                 "Blais-Lago", "ln(VAP)", "$ln(VAP)^{2}$", "\\% White", 
                  "\\% Over 65", "\\% Bach. +", "\\% OOH", 
                  "ln(MedianIncome)", "District-wide", "Open Seat Contested", 
                 "Uncontested Seat", "Teacher \\% X VAP")

teachTab1 <- apsrtable(teachRLM3, teachRLM4, se="robust", label="tab:teachmods", 
          model.names=c("RLM + District FE", "+ Interaction"), 
          caption = "Models of Unionization and Turnout in School Board Elections",
          notes = list(se.note, stars.note, "Year and district fixed effects omitted."),
          omitcoef=list("distid", 
                        expression(grep("distid", coefnames, value=TRUE)), 
                        "year", 
                        expression(grep("year", coefnames, value = TRUE))),
          coef.names = teachCoefnames,
          Sweave=FALSE)
@


Table \ref{tab:meInf} shows that there is some evidence to support that teacher 
share of the electorate has an effect on the turnout. Again, the control variables 
appear the same as before. THe key variables here are the teacher share of voters 
and its interaction with the voting age population. The main effects of the share 
of voters who are teachers is not significant, but it suggests the more teachers 
make up the voting age population the lower the turnout. THe interaction suggests 
that as the voting age population increases and the teacher share of voting age 
population increases, turnout decreases. 

This is counter to the theoretical expectations above, but it is understandable. 
If voters believe the school board election to be captured by a large interest 
group, they have  less of an incentive to vote. The threat of controlling the 
spring electorate for an interest group is enough to deter unorganized voters 
from participating. 


\begin{tiny}
<<teachInfMM, results='asis'>>=
teachCoefnames2 <- c("Intercept", "Teacher \\% VAP",  
                 "Blais-Lago", "Lag Turnout 2", "ln(VAP)", "\\% White", 
